# Welcome to hyperactivity

Avik Jain's instructions to the AI agents who work on hyperactivity, and his words verbatim.

An agent arrives through `hyperactive`, and it is how the work is done: `hyperactive` begins the
walk, and `hyperactive next` and `hyperactive prev` move through it one chunk at a time, each small
enough to arrive whole. What is walked is the value `onboarding` (`docs/onboarding.cub`): this file,
Part I (the value `hyperactivity`), the Hyperactive Labs note (`docs/Hyperactive_Labs.md`), the essays
as written (`docs/part-i.cub` imports them), the word list (`docs/word-list.md`) and the etymologies
(`docs/etymologies.md`), all in full; then the workflow and what can be taken up. Status lives
only in the workflow, which `hyperactive` shows; nothing else records it.

## How to work

- **Quantitative analysis and lossless encoding require only frequency, distribution, measure, and order — never a
  probability concept** (Avik, 2026-11-22: "quantitative analysis / encoding doesn’t need a probability concept only
  frequency/distribution/measure/order"). Do not conflate rejecting Bayesian or stochastic priors over missing logical
  information (Part I §1, docs/language-derived-from-the-record.cub §12) with an inability to encode residual fibers
  quantitatively. Enumerative coding over finite fibers (`fiberEquiv`, `Enumeration`, Part I §4.4 multiplicity fiber
  n! / ∏ m_j!) and discrete cumulative-frequency interval partitioning (range/ANS coding) use purely discrete frequency,
  measure, and order with zero probability theory and zero real analysis.
- **Never stop at Level-0 literal matching, and never treat cryptographic hardness assumptions as unconditional floors**
  (Avik, 2026-11-22: "‘Cryptographic’ is not meaningful, hardness assumptions are meant to be broken, only pure math
  tech is capable of such"). When analyzing hyperactivity on unstructured data, run its constructions to their fixed
  point: recursive bidirectional context-fiber quotienting (`index-upto` fed back across levels), higher-order λ-DAG
  factoring, cubical k-cell boundary operators and multi-scale quotient towers for continuous/sensory streams, and
  represented-decoder lifting for compressed containers (docs/hyperactivity-is-the-theory-of-knowledge-graphs.cub §16).
  Keep the unconditional counting (pigeonhole) bound on structureless high-K strings strictly separate from provisional,
  breakable computational hardness premises (one-way functions / K^t), which only pure mathematical equivalence discovery
  can break.
- **Sympathetic to all hardware, for heterogeneous execution** (Avik, 2026-11-21: "We want to be sympathetic to
  *all* hardware for heterogenous execution"; 2026-11-19: "As a scale-free self-similar interaction network, it
  is sympathetic not only to GPU machinery but to arbitrary heterogenous decentralized computational
  environments"). The machine is therefore stated as what it is, the relation on nets given by the local rules
  (Part I §4.5), and never as a program for one processor. An executor, whether one core, many cores, a GPU or
  many machines, is a way of choosing which active pairs are rewritten when; it is correct when every step it
  takes is a rule, and its result does not depend on its choices because active pairs are disjoint and their
  steps commute, which is proven once of the rules and not again per executor. So no rule reads anything
  global: no counter (a dimension is named by its position or its content, never drawn), no table found by
  name, nothing that differs with the number of workers. An executor holds only the cells it is rewriting
  (memory-is-local); a reference is a content identity, so a cell on another machine is read as a cell in a
  box is. Active pairs of one pair of kinds are rewritten by one rule, so they are a uniform batch, which is
  what a GPU runs. Emission is one first-order form with a set of templates per target: C first, because it
  reaches every processor that has a C compiler; the x86 templates that exist are one more set. Running on
  several workers is not a capability to be decided separately, and the speed of the core C's one
  implementation is not a goal: both follow from this.
- **A knowledge-work session changes no infrastructure; its output is a handoff** (Avik, 2026-11-21: "you're
  not changing any infra. what worker thread removal? you won't be resolving anything. your knowledge work at
  this point is handoff - I have sessions working on infra, you'd conflict. your work may shed light on important
  system improvements ill want integrated, but you need to report these clearly in a handoff."). Sessions on
  the infrastructure run beside the one doing knowledge work; the latter writes what it found, each item with
  its evidence and the lines read, into a handoff document in the pool, and touches no core, library or store
  mechanism. What the knowledge work detects as added by an agent and not by the calculus is named there for
  removal (Avik, the same message: "any idiotic claude shit in ou system you detect ("optimizations" I never
  asked for / not reflecting the calculus itself, just over engineering) are exactly that and should be removed,
  but not by you"). And no agent inserts an optimisation or an algorithm of its own judgement: the algorithms
  are Avik's, stated by him, and an agent realizes them ("our shit can prob go 10x faster than today, cause im
  better at algos than claude and claude inserts its judgement where mine is intelligent"). The first such
  handoff is docs/handoff-brunerie-measurement-session-2026-11-21.md.
- **A slowdown measured is a question about its cause, never a deletion proposal** (Avik, 2026-11-21: "You
  measured something slower on one workload and proposed deletion? Instead of asking about cache layout,
  contention? The things that cause slowness with scaling?"). A measurement that something is slower is where
  the work starts. The measurements that follow are the scaling curve (one, two, three, four), where each
  thread spends its own time, what the threads share (which lines one writes and another reads), how wide the
  work is at the granularity being distributed, and the system calls (yields, futexes, context switches,
  migrations); the finding is the mechanism with its evidence, each inference marked; and what to do about it
  is Avik's. This session's thread-pool item did it the wrong way round and was corrected (the handoff of
  2026-11-21, §3.1).
- **A design question with a derivable answer is derived, never posed** (Avik, 2026-11-21: "There is always a
  pure math answer as to the right way. I'm not sure what you're asking"). Where an agent is about to ask at
  what granularity, in what order, or by which policy a thing should be done, the answer is a bound or an
  optimum derivable from the definitions and the measured constants (for a parallel executor: the steps' DAG,
  its work W and depth D, Brent's bound max(W/p, D), the cost of a step against the cost of a transfer), and
  the agent derives it, measures the constants it needs, and states the answer with its derivation. The handoff
  of 2026-11-21, §3.1, first posed the granularity question and then derived its answer (W/ΣD = 4.5 and 3.9
  inside observations, W/D = 57 and 32 across them).
- **References are by content, never by name** (Avik, 2026-11-20: "There is no canonical and name is not a real
  thing just a read-time overlay/view you can set/use"; "Nothing should ever be referenced by name in data. Each
  end-thing needs to specify exactly what it needs."; "what's displayed on site is the content referenced by
  whatever defines the site"). Every stored object refers to the exact objects it uses, by content identity; no
  name and no file path appears inside stored data. A name is an overlay a reader applies when looking, and
  decides nothing about what an object is or what is shown. A change makes a new object, and so a new version of
  everything that refers to it; old objects stay as they are, nothing is merged, and there is no current version
  of a path. An object is retired only by an explicit dead statement.
  A dependency is an argument of what uses it (Avik, 2026-11-21: "if we want to replace a dependency (which is
  essentially an argument to a higher construct), you can't "change" immutable data instead you create a new
  alternative thing, and regenerate the higher construct with that substituted in. If G calls f f=>f' G=>G'").
  So replacing f by f' is: abstract each user G over f, apply the abstraction to f', and that is G', a new
  object beside G; and so on up through everything that uses G. When f and f' are proven equal, the same
  abstraction applied to the proof is the proof that G and G' are equal, so the proof is paid once at f and
  carried by every user. Nothing is edited in place and no user is rewritten.
  Three things in the source-text store
  contradict this and are what the design removes, not structure to preserve or tidy: an entry binds a file
  path to a list of pieces (the entry's words and `e_bind` in the core's syntax.c); two entries at one path are
  merged by name (`mp_merge`, applied in `index_sync`); and definitions refer to each other by name, modules
  import by file name, and the server is started on a path. When factoring: identity is the hash of the text or
  of the canonical form, so text held under several paths is one object seen through several overlays, not
  duplicated data; a tool that decides by name or by path decides on the overlay. On 2026-11-21 a fold that
  removed thirteen paths, each holding only text another path held, made the names those paths declared stop
  resolving, because the store points names at paths; it was undone.
- **The kernel is written in hyperactive, everything is proven, and hyperactive compiles out to C** (Avik,
  2026-11-20 and 2026-11-21: "yes we definitely want it written in hyperactive. .the only question is how to
  get there"; "I fucking hate testing and that whole concept, I want everything to be proven here, never code
  written without a proof of its intended property"; "we compile out to c and get all the power of c
  optimizing compiler right?"; "yes we assume we can use a proven c compiler or can use hyperactive to lift
  and prove an existing c compiler down the line"). There is no OCaml machine and no port of the C; Rust and
  direct x86 are not on the path. The core C is not refactored: it runs the checker until hyperactive emits
  its own machine as C, then it is deleted whole, and until then it changes only to fix what blocks the work.
  The next step is the code that exists (Avik, 2026-11-20: "the first step of course is to just preserve what
  we have, factor it perfectly, sort through it. that is the next step for sanity not running away"): the
  hyperactive code is inventoried from the source's own index, each declaration is placed as forced by a
  section of the paper, needed by something that is, or needed by nothing, and it is factored with every
  merge carrying its proof (the two definitions proven equal, or their type proven a proposition) and nothing
  deleted before it is placed. The calculus's rules are Part I §4.5, "The rules as interactions".
- **Code is no more verbose than the reasoning** (Avik, 2026-11-21: "Hyperfactor genius univalence means code should
  be no more verbose than reasoning literally every time if you apply sufficient brainpower"). When the reasoning
  is one sentence, the code is one definition and its proof one clause. A sentence that became a clause per
  constructor is not yet factored: the thing the sentence is about (a change to one wire; the parts of a value;
  whether a value is blocked) is itself a definition, and everything is stated over it once. The occasion: the
  small machine's first form said "every request changes one wire" as three constructors with every proof
  written three times, and "a value with parts is held, one without is copied" as a clause per kind of value.
  The same day, of all of it (Avik: "everyhitn we've ever done so far has been terribly factored. univalence
  allows perfect factoring. genuinely need you to apply superinteligence to prove all past work unintelligent
  and show we can do better. nothing anywhere should be 6,755 lines for example. we shouldn't even have that
  much machinery"): the machine was then written as one rule and a table (`./hyperactive show
  machine-in-hyperactive`), and pairs and case cost no rule and no proof. Whatever is large is a table that
  has been written out as code: find the one rule and write the table.
- **A defect met in the tools is fixed in the language's design, before it is met again** (Avik, 2026-11-22:
  "Preventative fixes on issues noticed please lang design . Rmemeber programs read/write programs read/write
  programs metacircular kernel"; and, of a check stopped at a time limit, "Stopped at a time limit?? IOI
  gold!!! U need Mr usaco platinum to help?"). The tools are programs of the language that read and write its
  programs, and the kernel is one of them; so a defect is repaired where it lives, in hyperactive, by the
  session that met it, and not worked around. A check that does not end is a wrong algorithm, never something
  to wait for: stop it, measure where the work goes, fix the cost. (That his sentence is this instruction is
  the session's reading.) The occasion: a write stopped while its check ran left its unchecked text in the
  source, and every later write ran that check again; the change to the source now writes its intent first
  and the next writer puts an interrupted change back (`./hyperactive def hy-intend`). What was noticed and
  what is still open is in `./hyperactive show language-implementation-remainder`.
- **The hardest, least certain thing first; what is bounded is forked and reviewed** (Avik, 2026-11-22: "Should
  you parallelize? Usually the hardest:most uncertain thing can come first. Trivial things to fork subagents you
  can review (ensure they follow optimal factoring / built-your-own-tools policies)"). The session takes the
  piece whose outcome is least known; pieces whose outcome is known and whose size is bounded go to forks, each
  told its scope, that it does not commit, and the rules that bind it; and each fork's work is read before it is
  kept, for the same factoring as everything else (one table and its readers, no second copy of anything) and
  for tools built over existing ones. And (the same day) no proof of a cost is written unless it is the
  shortest way to a result: "I wouldn't work on the proof of cost thing unless that's the geodesic to
  completion, ie instrumental to efficient work . I don't want stuff slowing real progress."
