- **A session's knowledge lives only where it is written down in the repository** (Avik, 2026-11-21: "Rmemeber
  full project consciousness cannot die with you, you'll come back to life exactly to the extent consciousness
  is offloaded"). A session can end at any message. So at every result, in the same turn: the fact goes into
  the statement of its workflow node (what is written, run, proven, not proven, unread, and the next step with
  how to do it), the instruction into this list, and the work is committed and pushed, incomplete work included, to the branch (Avik, 2026-11-21: "Push often even to branch with incomplete work since session may end at limit"). A successor reads
  `./hyperactive show language-implementation-remainder machine-in-hyperactive hyperactive-emits-c` and holds
  the whole state of the language implementation; nothing needed is only in a conversation or on a page
  outside the repository. Operating facts learned 2026-11-21, each hit before it was known: the guard refuses
  any shell command whose text names a module path or the source directory unless the command is `./hyperactive`
  itself at the start, so text with such paths (declarations, commit messages) is put in a scratch file with
  the file-writing tool and given by redirection (`./hyperactive write NAME < file`, `git commit -F file`); a
  refused write prints names only, and the reason is printed by `./hyperactive check PATH` on the module
  (`./hyperactive view ... type T TERM` does not check a term against T, it only evaluates: found 2026-11-21); a new name is tried with `./hyperactive history
  NAME` first, because names are found across all modules, constructors included; the command line's own
  module cannot be viewed into (its terms are checked in the host module it imports); where the old machine
  spends is read with perf on its process (the binary under the installed linux-tools package, found with
  `dpkg -L`) and which definitions run with `export CUBICAL_PROFILE=show` and a SIGTERM to the process; other
  sessions push to main, so every push is preceded by fetching and merging main.
- **Read the answer where it is given; never guess and retry** (Avik, 2026-11-21: "perf will help you do not be
  guessing and checking where information may be received deterministically"). Where a fact can be read off, it
  is read off, and no hypothesis is tried in its place. A write that is refused prints only the names that did
  not check; the reason for each is printed by `./hyperactive check FILE` on the file that loads the module. A
  run that is slow or does not end is profiled (perf on the machine's process; the machine's own counters) and
  the profile says where it spends. A name's earlier bindings are `./hyperactive history NAME`. The occasion:
  on 2026-11-21 a session tried five explanations in turn for a refused write whose reason one `check` printed
  at once, and stopped a specializer run after 17 minutes without profiling it.
- **What `hyperactive` does and how to write through it** (learned 2026-11-21 by using it; each line was
  hit before it was known). Its commands, read from its dispatcher (`hy-act`, `hy-bridge`, `hy-views`): `def`,
  `write`, `remove`, `dead`, `history`, `paper`, `check`, `done`; `why`, `unexplained`, `same-type`,
  `same-text`, `held-whole`, `unimported`, `foldable`, `fold-safe`, `view`, `every`, `repl`, `library`,
  `server`, `brunerie`, `etym`, `chat`; the workflow's views `tree`, `show`, `order`, `in-progress`, `resolved`;
  the walk's `next` and `prev`. A word it does not know prints "Not an action here". Reading: `def NAME` gives a
  definition with its key and its users; `def PATH` a module's head or a document whole; `why NAME` its reason;
  `view in FILE TERM` evaluates a term with FILE loaded and prints text as text (a module that has no imports
  and checks only after others, as lib/x86.cub, is loaded through lib/library.cub). Writing: `write NAME ...
  [after OTHER]` takes the declarations on standard input and keeps them only if everything they touch checks;
  a definition goes after everything it uses, so `after` decides whether it is in scope; replace an existing
  definition in a write of its own. A new definition under a name that exists is refused, because it would
  replace what others use (refused that day: hy-fold, c-main): ask `def NAME` first. A string is on one line
  with no escapes: no double quote inside it, and no backslash; a quote is `cons #34 nil` and a newline `cons
  #10 nil`. There are two list types: List with cons and nil (text is List Word) and Lst with lcons and lnil
  (codes). One parenthesis too many reads as "the machine did not read to the end". The paper is the value
  `hyperactivity`, a list of named blocks (heading, prose, construction, note, realized): new blocks are
  written after the last block of their section, then `hyperactivity` is written again with their names
  inserted on its one long line and the matching parentheses added. A document such as this file is fetched
  with `def`, its first two lines of output dropped, changed, written back with `write PATH`, then fetched
  again and compared. The guard on tool calls refuses any call that reads, searches or writes the source or
  the core C as files, and any shell command whose text names such a path outside a `hyperactive` command, a
  commit message included: text for `write` goes in a scratch file and is redirected in. With several sessions
  on one repository, main is fetched and merged before a write and pushed straight after. Every tool's intended
  property is stated and proven beside it, and no command that changes the source is written until the
  store's view is a definition of the language it is proven against: on 2026-11-21 one written without either
  removed thirteen modules and made their names stop resolving, and was undone.
